gainst the new source table. The resolver case passes its controls, then fails \"no such table: random_rowids\" in the old reader. The fresh-out job case exits 0 and fails reading `random_ann_ids` (\"no such table\"). In the open case, the control fails `usable is not None`: the old count does not see `random_ann_ids`. In the refresh case, the old code serves the `random_rowids` file (`owner.random_cache_db is None` fails). In the job old-shape case, the control fails because the old job rebuilds a `random_ann_ids` `--out` (inode 7522365 -> 7522372).\nValidateTests [\"tests/tmp/test_probe_42_phase2.py\", \"-s\"] (a probe against today's code) printed the following.\n- METADATA {1560750181575914601: ('A','a.example','title A'), 6839237213464704732: ('C','a.example','title C')} and ROWID1 [(1,'D'),(2,'C'),(3,'B'),(4,'A')]. So phase 1's `fetch_metadata`, on the sync-whitelist schema with embedding x'00', dim 1 and nsfw 0, resolves by `ann_id`, drops 1 and an unknown id, and D's embedding holds rowid 1 when inserted in reverse.\n- CAP 6 Counter({'c1':2,'c2':2,'c0':2}): the filtered cap of 2 per author over this 20-row, 3-channel source gives 6, two per channel. Observed on the rowid version of the same scan.\n- WRAP 5 [(1, 20)] [1, 2, 3, 4, 20]: an unfiltered populate calls `random.randint` once with (min, max), and a start scripted to `high` gives the max plus the 4 smallest (wrap-around). Observed on the rowid version.\n- MESSAGES ['random cache unusable path=<.../e/random-cache.db> reason=empty', 'random cache unusable path=<.../o/random-cache.db> reason=no_table']: the exact log format, with the path rendered as str(Path).\nThe probe file `tests/tmp/test_probe_42_phase2.py` is still on disk because I have no delete tool, and it needs removing. Not observable until phase 2 lands: the new code's positive outcomes (its `random_ann_ids` writes, `fetch_random_ann_ids`, and the `ann_id`-ordered window). These expectations rest on the plan's Step-6 spec and on the rowid-version observations above; the phase-2 green run will confirm them.\n</probes>\n\n<unassertable>\nnone\n</unassertable>",
+    "self_check:tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase2.py": "<rows>\n<row clause=\"C1\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase2.py:142-144 \u2014 for both the unfiltered and the uncapped filtered populate over the 20-row source: `built == 20`, `sorted(fetch_random_ann_ids(cache, 100)) == SOURCE_IDS`, `_tables(cache_path) == {\"random_ann_ids\"}`.</assertion>\n<expected>20; the 20 computed `compute_ann_id(label, \"a.example\")` values (distinct, none in 1..20, which the control at 137-138 showed passing in the run); tables {\"random_ann_ids\"}.</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>A populate that still writes `video_rowid`s (or reads `e.rowid` while inserting into the renamed table) stores 1..20, which the control proves disjoint from SOURCE_IDS, so line 143 fails. One that keeps `random_rowids` in the file, or creates both tables, fails line 144. Today it fails at line 120 because `fetch_random_ann_ids` does not exist.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n<row clause=\"C1\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase2.py:153-154 \u2014 with `random_cache.random.randint` scripted to return its upper bound, an unfiltered populate of 5 returns 5 and holds {max ann_id, the four smallest ann_ids}.</assertion>\n<expected>5, and the set {SOURCE_IDS[-1], *SOURCE_IDS[:4]}. A probe ran the plan's `ann_id >= start ORDER BY ann_id LIMIT 5` plus the `ann_id < start` wrap over this source with start = MAX(ann_id) and got exactly that set.</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>A window still drawn over `rowid` (start = MAX(rowid) = 20) holds rowid-ordered rows 20,1,2,3,4. Their ann_ids are not the largest plus the four smallest, so line 154 fails. If the window had no wrap it would hold 1 id, and line 153 would fail.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n<row clause=\"C1\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase2.py:160-163 \u2014 a filtered populate capped at 2 per author returns 6 and serves 6 distinct ids. They are a subset of SOURCE_IDS, 2 per channel c0/c1/c2.</assertion>\n<expected>6; 6 distinct source ann_ids; Counter {\"c0\": 2, \"c1\": 2, \"c2\": 2}. A probe of today's rowid scan over this source showed the cap logic giving 6 = 2+2+2.</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>A filtered scan that still collects `e.rowid` gives values 1..20. They are not in SOURCE_IDS, so line 161 fails, and `CHANNEL_BY_ID` would KeyError at 163. A scan that dropped the author cap after the column change gives 20, and line 160 fails.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n<row clause=\"C1\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase2.py:172-173 \u2014 `fetch_random_ann_ids` on a hand-seeded `random_ann_ids` cache [ids 7, 2, 11] returns them in position order, and `[]` at limit 0.</assertion>\n<expected>[SOURCE_IDS[7], SOURCE_IDS[2], SOURCE_IDS[11]]; [].</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>A fetch still reading `random_rowids` raises \"no such table\". A fetch that orders by `ann_id` instead of `position` returns [2, 7, 11]. Either way line 172 fails. Today the name is missing (AttributeError at 172).</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n<row clause=\"C1\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase2.py:204 \u2014 `fetch_random_rows_from_cache` over a cache [ann C, 1, ann A, unknown, ann D] returns (video_id, instance_domain, title) for C, A, D in that order.</assertion>\n<expected>[(\"C\",\"a.example\",\"title C\"), (\"A\",\"a.example\",\"title A\"), (\"D\",\"a.example\",\"title D\")]. A probe of `fetch_metadata` on this fixture with those five ids returned exactly C, A and D keyed by ann_id, with 1 and the unknown id absent.</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>A reader still on `fetch_random_rowids` fails on \"no such table: random_rowids\", and that is today's red at random_cache.py:333. A reader that resolved cached values by rowid would return D for the value 1 (control at 193 shows rowid 1 is D) and would drop the real ann_ids.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n<row clause=\"C1\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase2.py:213-217 \u2014 `precompute-random-rowids.py --size 20` on a fresh `--out` exits 0. The file holds positions 1..20, its ann_id column sorted equals SOURCE_IDS, and its only table is `random_ann_ids`.</assertion>\n<expected>returncode 0; [1..20]; SOURCE_IDS; {\"random_ann_ids\"}.</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>Today's job writes `random_rowids` of rowids, so `_cache_rows` raises \"no such table: random_ann_ids\" (the run's red at line 108 via 214). A job writing rowids into the new table fails 216. One leaving a `random_rowids` table beside it fails 217.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n<row clause=\"C2\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase2.py:232-233 \u2014 `open_random_cache_if_usable` on a 20-row `random_rowids` file returns None and logs `random cache unusable path=<old> reason=no_table`. Controls at 237-243 show the same opener serving a 3-id `random_ann_ids` cache and reporting an empty one as `reason=empty`.</assertion>\n<expected>None, and the `reason=no_table` message present in caplog.messages.</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>Today's opener counts `random_rowids` and serves the old file: the run shows a sqlite3.Connection at line 232. An opener that took any table holding rows as usable would do the same. One that rejected the file through an open error would log `reason=OperationalError\u2026` and fail 233.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n<row clause=\"C2\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase2.py:252, 256-258, 262 \u2014 the old file opens as None. `refresh_random_cache` from it returns True, the active path's inode changes, its only table is `random_ann_ids`, and the owner's swapped handle serves exactly SOURCE_IDS.</assertion>\n<expected>None; True; a new inode; {\"random_ann_ids\"}; SOURCE_IDS. A probe of today's refresh showed a True return, a new inode and a non-None owner handle after a swap from owner=None.</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>Today the old cache is served: the run shows a Connection at line 252. A build that still wrote `random_rowids` would swap in a file whose tables are {\"random_rowids\"}, failing 258. A swap check still on the old table would leave the old file and return False, failing 256.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n<row clause=\"C2\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase2.py:277-284 \u2014 the job without flags at `--size 20` on a 20-row `random_rowids` `--out` exits 0 and the file gets a new inode. It holds positions 1..20 over exactly SOURCE_IDS, only `random_ann_ids`, and the directory holds only `--out`. Control at 286-288 shows a 20-id `random_ann_ids` `--out` kept, with the same inode and bytes.</assertion>\n<expected>returncode 0; a new inode; [1..20]; SOURCE_IDS; {\"random_ann_ids\"}; [out name]. Probes showed today's job keeping a 20-row file with the same inode and bytes, and a `--refresh` rebuild leaving a new inode with only `random-cache.db` in the directory.</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>Today's keep check counts `random_rowids` (20 \u2265 20) and keeps the old file: the run shows `assert 7522712 != 7522712` at line 279. A job that rebuilds unconditionally passes 279-284 but fails the keep control at 287. One that rebuilt in place would keep the inode, and one leaving its temp file behind fails 284.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n</rows>\n\n<answers>\n1. Whole claim: no gap. Every docstring bullet has assertions: populate in both modes (142-144), the wrap (153-154), the cap (160-163), fetch order and limit 0 (172-173), cache resolution (204), the fresh job (213-217), the opener (232-243), refresh (252-262), and job rebuild/keep (277-288).\n2. Absence only: no. Line 232 (None) carries the positive `reason=no_table` log at 233, plus the serving and `reason=empty` controls at 237-243. Line 252's None is followed by the positive swap and serve at 256-262. The keep (287-288, unchanged inode and bytes) sits in the same test as the positive rebuild of the old file at 279-284.\n3. Echoed literal: no. Expected ids come from `compute_ann_id` on the fixture's labels, and production must read them back from `video_embeddings.ann_id`; the test performs no draw. For example, reverting the INSERT to `row[\"rowid\"]` turns 143/216/282 red, and reverting `random_ann_ids_count`'s table name turns 232 red.\n4. One value: no. Populate is read unfiltered, filtered-uncapped, filtered-capped and with a scripted wrap start. The opener is read on old, current and empty files. The job is read on fresh, old and current `--out`s.\n5. The double: no project module is doubled. `random_cache.random` is replaced by stdlib randint/shuffle to script the window start. The owner is a SimpleNamespace attribute holder carrying real sqlite connections and real `threading.Lock`s, the duck-typed `owner: Any` that `refresh_random_cache`/`swap_readonly_connection` take. `sync-whitelist.py`, `fetch_metadata`, `random_cache` and the job all run for real.\n6. It collects: yes. The `--collect-only -q` summary I was handed says \"no tests\", but the full runs printed \"collected 10 items\" (the parametrized test counts twice) and reached the test bodies, so every import resolves. Helper arguments match the real signatures: `populate_random_cache(src, cache, size, refresh, filtered, max_per_instance, max_per_author)` and `refresh_random_cache(source, active, size, filtered, mpi, mpa, owner, stop_event)`. The one name that does not exist yet, `fetch_random_ann_ids`, is the phase's own rename.\n7. Observed, not predicted: as far as anything can be observed before the phase exists. A probe (tests/tmp/probe_42_phase2.py) showed:\n   - `fetch_metadata` on this fixture returns C, A and D keyed by ann_id and drops 1 and the unknown id;\n   - the plan's ann_id window SQL with start = MAX(ann_id) gives {max, four smallest};\n   - SOURCE_IDS are 20 distinct values disjoint from 1..20;\n   - today's capped filter gives 6 = 2+2+2;\n   - today's job keeps a 20-row `--out` with the same inode and bytes, and a rebuild leaves a new inode and only `random-cache.db` in the directory;\n   - today's refresh returns True with a new inode and a non-None owner handle.\n   What cannot be observed until phase 2 exists is the new table and column names themselves; those come from the plan's spec of `random_ann_ids(position, ann_id)` and `fetch_random_ann_ids`. I could not delete the probe with my tools, so I emptied it; it is left in tests/tmp as an empty file that collects nothing and should be removed.\n   Rewrite in response to 9: the first run's reds in two tests were CONTROL failures, so in each I moved the C2 assertions ahead of their controls:\n   - line 234: `usable is not None` \u2192 `assert None is not None`;\n   - line 276: `current.stat().st_ino == current_inode` \u2192 `7522536 == 7522529`, because today's job rebuilds a `random_ann_ids` file it cannot count.\n   The controls are unchanged and now come after the claim assertions.\n8. Red, not green: yes, it fails. The rerun printed \"10 failed\" and \"[exit status 1]\".\n9. Red for the right reason: yes. Every failure is at the phase's own rename or claim, and no control fails any more:\n   - lines 120/172: `AttributeError: module 'data.random_cache' has no attribute 'fetch_random_ann_ids'` (the phase's rename, not yet made), \u00d75 tests;\n   - random_cache.py:333 via test line 199: `sqlite3.OperationalError: no such table: random_rowids`, after the controls at 193-194 passed;\n   - line 108 via 214: `no such table: random_ann_ids`, after `returncode == 0` at 213 passed;\n   - line 232 (C2): `assert <sqlite3.Connection object \u2026> is None`;\n   - line 252 (C2): `assert <sqlite3.Connection object \u2026> is None`;\n   - line 279 (C2): `assert 7522712 != 7522712`, after `rebuilt.returncode == 0` at 277 passed.\n10. Observed expected output: each row's `expected` matches what the probes showed. Where the run reached a judging assertion, it contradicted only today's code, in the predicted way: a Connection at 232/252 and an unchanged inode at 279. Nothing in the run contradicted an expected value.\n</answers>\n\n<exemptions>\nnone\n</exemptions>",
+    "step_7_implement:2": "<changes>\n### `engine/server/data/random_cache.py`\n- The cache table is now `random_ann_ids (position INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, ann_id INTEGER NOT NULL)`. Before it was `random_rowids (position, video_rowid)`. `RANDOM_CACHE_CHECK_SQL` names the new table, so a swap can only install a file in the new shape.\n- `random_rowids_count` is renamed `random_ann_ids_count` and looks for `random_ann_ids`. A file holding only `random_rowids` returns None. So `open_random_cache_if_usable` logs `reason=no_table` for it through its existing path, with no new code. A `rat-tail:` comment says the old cache is detected by table name, not by shape; if the table is ever reshaped under the same name, the upgrade is a `PRAGMA table_info` check.\n- `populate_random_cache` now works on `video_embeddings.ann_id` instead of `rowid`:\n  - the stats query takes `MIN/MAX(ann_id)`;\n  - the unfiltered window runs from `ann_id >= start` and wraps to `ann_id < start`;\n  - the filtered `scan_range` selects, filters, orders and advances on `e.ann_id`;\n  - the local list is renamed `ann_ids`, and both inserts write `random_ann_ids (position, ann_id)`.\n  \n  A comment above the start draw says why the window is still a uniform sample: hashed ids are spread evenly across the range.\n- `fetch_random_rowids` is renamed `fetch_random_ann_ids` and reads `SELECT ann_id FROM random_ann_ids ORDER BY position`. Docstrings for `build_random_cache`, `open_random_cache_if_usable` and the fetch now say ANN ids.\n\n### `engine/server/data/random_videos.py`\n- It imports `fetch_random_ann_ids`. In `fetch_random_rows_from_cache` the locals are now `ann_ids`, and they go straight to `fetch_metadata`, which already resolves by `ann_id` since Phase 1. The docstring says \"ANN-id cache\" and \"unseen ANN id\".\n\n### `engine/server/db/jobs/precompute-random-rowids.py`\n- The keep check now uses `random_ann_ids_count`. An old `random_rowids` `--out` counts as 0, so it is rebuilt by rename rather than kept.\n- The description is now \"Precompute random ANN-id cache.\" and the `--size` help \"ANN ids to sample.\".\n- The file name and arguments are unchanged.\n\n### `engine/server/api/server_config.py`\n- The comment on `DEFAULT_RANDOM_CACHE_SIZE` now says \"random ANN ids\".\n\n### `tests/active/test_precompute_random_rowids.py`\n- These are the plan's fixture changes, done in place as Phase 1 did for its tests:\n  - `engine/server` is added to `sys.path` and `compute_ann_id` is imported;\n  - the source table gains `ann_id`, filled with computed ids;\n  - `_seed_cache` and `_cache_rows` use `random_ann_ids(position, ann_id)`;\n  - the \"rowids 1..20\" expectations become `SOURCE_IDS`, the sorted computed ids;\n  - the docstrings are updated.\n- I did not add the plan's new `old_shape` case. The checkpoint already pins it, and Phase 1 left that copy for when the checkpoint is promoted.\n\n### `tests/active/test_db.py`\n- Imports `compute_ann_id` and `fetch_random_ann_ids`.\n- `SEEDED_ROWIDS` is now the computed ids of v7, v3 and v11, and `SEEDED_ROWS` is built from them.\n- `CHECK_SQL` and `_seed_cache` use `random_ann_ids`, and the source gains `ann_id`.\n- `_served_rowids` is renamed `_served_ann_ids`, and the docstring is updated.\n\n### `tests/active/test_random_videos.py`\n- `NSFW_EMBEDDINGS_TABLE` gains `ann_id INTEGER NOT NULL`. Both embedding inserts (`nsfw_conn` and `_video_db`) carry `compute_ann_id(label, NSFW_HOST)`.\n- `_video_db` returns a map from label to ann_id.\n- `_cache_owner` writes a `random_ann_ids` table, wraps `random_cache.fetch_random_ann_ids`, and monkeypatches `random_videos.fetch_random_ann_ids`.\n- The docstring and one comment are updated. Test function names are unchanged, so test ids stay the same.\n\n### `tests/active/test_random_cache.py`\n- Imports `compute_ann_id` and adds `SOURCE_IDS`. `SEEDED_ROWIDS`/`SEEDED_ROWS` become computed ids, and `_source_db` gains `ann_id`.\n- Every `random_rowids`/`video_rowid` read, write, delete and drop now uses `random_ann_ids`/`ann_id`. That includes the engines test's count and its lock probe.\n- Expectations of \"rowids 1..20\" become `SOURCE_IDS`.\n- Helpers renamed: `_cache_rowids` to `_cache_ann_ids`, `_served_rowids` to `_served_ann_ids`, `_feed_rowids` to `_feed_ann_ids`.\n- The Engine helpers now select `ann_id`: `_feed_ann_ids` looks it up by `(video_id, instance_domain)`, and `_seed_servable_cache` selects `e.ann_id ORDER BY e.ann_id`.\n- The `no_table` probe of `open_random_cache_if_usable` now writes an old-format `random_rowids` file with rows, replacing the unrelated `other` table.\n- The module docstring is updated.\n\n### `tests/config.json`\n- `test_db.py` now maps `random_cache.py` and `ann_ids.py`.\n- `test_random_videos.py` now maps `random_cache.py` and `ann_ids.py`.\n- `test_random_cache.py` and `test_precompute_random_rowids.py` now map `ann_ids.py`.\n</changes>\n\n<inner_tests>\nnone\n</inner_tests>\n\n<unanticipated>\nnone\n</unanticipated>\n\n<awaiting_operator>\nnone\n</awaiting_operator>",
+    "author:tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase3.py": "<exemptions>\nnone\n</exemptions>\n\n<items>\n<item id=\"D12\">\n<disposition>justified</disposition>\n<what>I narrowed the docstring to what the test actually does. Line 6 used to say the job runs \"with explicit tmp paths so the served index is never touched\", which promised something about the served index that no assertion checks. It now reads \"the job is passed explicit tmp `--db-path`, `--index-path` and `--meta-path` arguments (setup, not asserted)\". That describes the arguments `_run_job` passes and claims nothing about `engine/server/db/`. The served index is not a must_prove clause. Writing to the requested paths is still carried by C1 (:76, :79 read the index back from `--index-path`, :80-84 read the sidecar from `--meta-path`). Writing nothing at those paths on refusal is carried by C2 (:105, :106).</what>\n</item>\n</items>\n\n<findings_addressed>\nClaim RECOMMENDATION 1 (D12 whole-claim, :6): taken in its narrow-the-sentence form. The docstring now describes the tmp path arguments as setup and no longer promises the served index is untouched. I did not add an assertion on `engine/server/db/`'s default index, because it would depend on whatever state that live file happens to be in.\nClaim RECOMMENDATION 2 (bounds: empty `ann_id` table, DB with no `video_embeddings`): not taken. Neither case is in must_prove, and both go beyond C1/C2.\nNo CRITICALs from either auditor.\n</findings_addressed>\n\n<rows>\n<row clause=\"C1\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase3.py:76 \u2014 the job exits 0 on the 64-row `ann_id` source</assertion>\n<expected>returncode 0, and an index exists at `--index-path`</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>A job that crashes or refuses a migrated DB, for example an `ann_id` guard that misfires. The returncode is non-zero.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n<row clause=\"C1\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase3.py:79 \u2014 the sorted `id_map` read back from the written index equals the sorted `SELECT ann_id FROM video_embeddings`</assertion>\n<expected>the 64 hashed `ann_id`s, e.g. `[189395026504963824, \u2026]`</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>Today's job keys the index by rowid, so the read-back is `[1, 2, \u2026, 64]` (re-observed this round, failing at :79). A dropped or duplicated row also fails full-list equality. The controls at :70-72 make sure rowids and ann_ids cannot overlap.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n<row clause=\"C1\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase3.py:81 \u2014 sidecar `id_source == \"video_embeddings.ann_id\"`</assertion>\n<expected>\"video_embeddings.ann_id\"</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>The current hard-coded `\"video_embeddings.rowid\"` reads `video_embeddings.rowid` here. So does a shared constant left at the rowid value, which would make :83 pass vacuously.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n<row clause=\"C1\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase3.py:83 \u2014 sidecar `id_source == ann_ids.ANN_ID_SOURCE`</assertion>\n<expected>equal to the module constant, whose value :81 pins</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>A job that writes its own literal instead of the shared constant, or a phase that never adds `ANN_ID_SOURCE`. The second raises AttributeError at run time.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n<row clause=\"C2\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase3.py:102 \u2014 the same run on the six-column table exits non-zero</assertion>\n<expected>a returncode other than 0</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>Today's job has no `ann_id` check and builds the six-column table anyway, giving `assert 0 != 0` (re-observed this round). The controls at :97-98 show the missing column is the only difference.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n<row clause=\"C2\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase3.py:103 \u2014 stderr contains `migrate-whitelist.py`</assertion>\n<expected>the refusal message names `migrate-whitelist.py`</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>A job that fails with a raw `sqlite3.OperationalError: no such column: ann_id`. It exits non-zero, but stderr never names the migration.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n<row clause=\"C2\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase3.py:105, :106 \u2014 neither `index.faiss` nor `index.faiss.json` exists in tmp_path</assertion>\n<expected>both files absent</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>A guard placed after `write_index` or after the sidecar write, or a job that writes both and then exits non-zero. The unguarded job, probed on this same DB, left both files there.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n</rows>\n\n<answers>\n1. No. The negative assertions at :105-106 are armed by the probe, which showed the unguarded job leaves both files at these paths. The control assertions at :97-98 pin the input. Since :102 must also turn red, a job that is absent or does nothing does not satisfy the test as a whole.\n2. No. :79 compares the index read back by a separate FAISS child against the DB's own `ann_id` column. Deleting the switch from rowid to `ann_id` in build-ann-index.py (lines ~250-252) turns it red. :81 is a literal, and deleting the job's `id_source` assignment turns it red.\n3. No. The ids are checked over 64 distinct values, sorted and compared in full. The sidecar value is pinned to an independent literal (:81) as well as to the constant (:83).\n4. No. There are no doubles: the real job and real FAISS run under the Engine interpreter.\n5. Yes, it collects. This round only changed docstring prose, and a re-run collected and ran 2 tests.\n6. Yes. Every expected shape came from earlier probe runs (rowid ids, the `id_map` read, the sidecar keys, files left by the unguarded job), and this round's run reproduced the same failures.\n7. Yes. Re-run after the edit: 2 failed. C1 fails at :79 with `[1, 2, 3, \u2026] == [189395026504\u2026]` and C2 fails at :102 with `assert 0 != 0`, both because the phase has not been built yet. All controls pass. Nothing needed rewriting. The probe files listed in my previous reply are still in tests/tmp/ and should be deleted.\n</answers>",
+    "self_check:tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase3.py": "<rows>\n<row clause=\"C1\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase3.py:79 \u2014 the sorted ids read back from the written index's `id_map` (child on the Engine interpreter) equal the sorted `SELECT ann_id FROM video_embeddings` of the source DB</assertion>\n<expected>The 64 DB `ann_id`s, each a 63-bit blake2b value (the first sorted one is 189395026504963824), none of them in 1..64</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>The current job adds with `rowid` (build-ann-index.py:250-252). The run showed the index ids as `[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, ...]` and failed with `At index 0 diff: 1 != 189395026504963824`. A build that drops or skips any row also fails here, because the set must match exactly.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n<row clause=\"C1\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase3.py:81 and :83 \u2014 sidecar `meta[\"id_source\"]` equals the literal `\"video_embeddings.ann_id\"` (the AC4 value that readers resolve), and also equals `ann_ids.ANN_ID_SOURCE`</assertion>\n<expected>`\"video_embeddings.ann_id\"`, which is also the value of `data.ann_ids.ANN_ID_SOURCE`</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>Two cases. If the ids are switched but the sidecar line build-ann-index.py:284 is left as `\"video_embeddings.rowid\"`, :81 reads `\"video_embeddings.rowid\"` and fails. If `ANN_ID_SOURCE` is defined with the wrong value, or never added to `data/ann_ids.py`, :83 fails, with a mismatch or an AttributeError. Line 76 shows the job exits 0 now, but the run stops at :79, so neither line is reached against the current code.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n<row clause=\"C2\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase3.py:102 and :103 \u2014 over the six-column table with no `ann_id`, the job exits non-zero and its stderr contains `migrate-whitelist.py`</assertion>\n<expected>A non-zero exit, with stderr carrying the old-shape message `... Run engine/server/db/jobs/migrate-whitelist.py on this database ...`. A probe ran `assert_video_embeddings_has_ann_id` on this same DB in a child on the Engine interpreter: CHILD_RC 1, NAMED True.</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>The current job has no guard. The run showed `assert 0 != 0`: it exited 0 after logging `writing index to .../index.faiss`. A job that only switches its query to `SELECT ann_id` and adds no guard exits non-zero on sqlite's `no such column: ann_id`, which never names `migrate-whitelist.py`, so :103 fails.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n<row clause=\"C2\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase3.py:105 and :106 \u2014 after the refused run, neither `index.faiss` nor `index.faiss.json` exists at the `--index-path` / `--meta-path` given</assertion>\n<expected>Both files are absent.</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>A job that runs the guard late writes before it refuses, for example after `faiss.write_index`, or after the meta write inside a finally. Probed against the unguarded job, this same DB gave `RC 0 INDEX True META True`: both files appear at exactly these paths. That proves the paths are live, and these assertions turn red under a write-then-refuse order.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n</rows>\n\n<answers>\n1. Whole claim \u2014 no. Every clause in the docstring has a carrier. C1: the id set is exact (:79), `id_source` is checked both as the literal and as `ANN_ID_SOURCE` (:81, :83), and `total` is checked (:84). C2: non-zero exit (:102), `migrate-whitelist.py` in stderr (:103), and no index or meta file afterwards (:105, :106). One limit: the fixture's `ann_id` column holds `compute_ann_id` values, so a job that recomputes the id instead of reading the column would also pass :79. That is still an exact match to the DB's `ann_id` set, which is what C1 states, so I did not change the fixture.\n2. Absence only \u2014 no. The absence assertions :105 and :106 are armed in two ways. First, :102 and :103 prove the job ran and refused. Second, a probe (`tests/tmp/probe_phase3_c2.py`) ran the unguarded job on this same six-column DB and printed `RC 0 INDEX True META True`, so these are the paths the job writes. The current run's stderr shows it too: `INFO writing index to .../index.faiss`. I added a one-line comment above :105 recording that observation.\n3. Echoed literal \u2014 no. :79 compares the ids FAISS read back from the written file with the DB column. The test does not transform anything; deleting the switch from rowid to ann_id at build-ann-index.py:250-252 turns it red, as the run shows. :81 pins the AC4 sidecar value as a literal, so a wrong constant cannot echo itself through :83. Line 284 (`\"id_source\": \"video_embeddings.rowid\"`) going unchanged is what turns :81 red.\n4. One value \u2014 no. The ids are read over 64 distinct keys and compared against all 64. `id_source` is a single constant by definition. `total` is a supporting check.\n5. The double \u2014 no doubles. Everything is real: the real job script, real FAISS on the Engine interpreter, and the real `data.ann_ids` module.\n6. It collects \u2014 yes, it collects cleanly. The run printed `collected 2 items`, matching the two tests written. The collect-only summary line read `no tests`, but that is the summary display for a collect-only run, not a failure: exit 0. `ann_ids.ANN_ID_SOURCE` is read as a module attribute on purpose, so collection does not depend on the constant this phase adds. Every helper argument (`ensure_video_embeddings_schema`, `compute_ann_id`, the job's CLI flags) exists in the current tree.\n7. Observed, not predicted \u2014 yes for every output value. The rowid ids `[1, 2, 3, ...]` and the ann_id `189395026504963824` come from the run. The guard's stderr naming `migrate-whitelist.py` and its non-zero exit on the Engine interpreter come from the probe (`CHILD_RC 1 NAMED True`). That the files are written at these paths also comes from the probe (`INDEX True META True`). The `id_source` literal is the required value from AC4 and ADR-0006, not a predicted output shape. Changes made: the C2 comment, plus the probe. There is no delete tool, so I emptied the probe file to zero bytes; it should be removed with tests/tmp.\n8. Red, not green \u2014 yes, red. `ValidateTests` printed `2 failed` and `[exit status 1]`.\n9. Red for the right reason \u2014 yes. Every control passed: :70-72 and :78 in C1, :97-98 in C2. Both failures are at clause assertions. C1 fails at `tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase3.py:79: AssertionError` with `assert [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, ...] == [189395026504...61365324, ...]` / `At index 0 diff: 1 != 189395026504963824`: the index is still keyed by rowid. C2 fails at `tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase3.py:102: AssertionError` with `assert 0 != 0`, after `INFO writing index to .../index.faiss`: there is no guard yet.\n10. Observed expected output \u2014 yes. Each row's `expected` and `wrong_implementation` matches what the run or the probe printed. The run contradicted nothing.\n</answers>\n\n<exemptions>\nnone\n</exemptions>",
+    "step_7_implement:3": "<changes>\n### `engine/server/data/ann_ids.py`\n- Added the module constant `ANN_ID_SOURCE = \"video_embeddings.ann_id\"`. It is the value an index keyed by ann_id writes as the sidecar's `id_source`.\n\n### `engine/server/db/jobs/build-ann-index.py`\n- Imports `ANN_ID_SOURCE` and `assert_video_embeddings_has_ann_id` from `data.ann_ids`.\n- `main()` calls `assert_video_embeddings_has_ann_id(conn)` straight after connecting, before `resolve_embedding_space`, training or any write. A `video_embeddings` table without `ann_id` therefore raises the existing RuntimeError, which names `engine/server/db/jobs/migrate-whitelist.py`. The job exits non-zero and writes no index file and no sidecar.\n- Renamed the field `EmbeddingRow.rowid` to `ann_id`, and `iter_embeddings` now unpacks `ann_id`. Both are used only in this file.\n- The add query is now `SELECT ann_id, embedding, embedding_dim FROM video_embeddings`, and `add_with_ids` gets the ann_ids as int64.\n- The sidecar's `id_source` is now `ANN_ID_SOURCE`, where it used to be the literal `\"video_embeddings.rowid\"`.\n- `fetch_training_samples` still samples with `rowid % step`. That only spreads the training sample out and is never used as an id. A `rat-tail:` comment there names the limit: a sparse rowid range under-fills the sample, and the fix would be `ORDER BY RANDOM()` or a stride over ann_id.\n</changes>\n\n<inner_tests>\nnone\n</inner_tests>\n\n<unanticipated>\nnone\n</unanticipated>\n\n<awaiting_operator>\nnone\n</awaiting_operator>",
+    "author:tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase4.py": "<exemptions>\nnone\n</exemptions>\n\n<items>\n<item id=\"C1c\">\n<disposition>fixed</disposition>\n<what>Added `test_refuses_a_near_miss_sidecar_no_legacy_value_uses_naming_it_and_build_ann_index`. It rewrites the real build's sidecar with `id_source: \"video_embeddings.ann_ids\"`, a value no legacy sidecar carries. The test asserts a RuntimeError at :98, and checks that the message names `video_embeddings.ann_ids` (:101) and `build-ann-index.py` (:102). A denylist (`if id_source in (\"video_embeddings.rowid\", None): raise`) accepts this value, so :98 reports DID NOT RAISE. A prefix match (`startswith(ANN_ID_SOURCE)`) fails the same way. Only the allowlist `!= ANN_ID_SOURCE` passes. I also added a docstring bullet describing this case.</what>\n</item>\n</items>\n\n<findings_addressed>\nClaim CRITICAL 1 (whole-claim at :84, \"any other\" tested at one value only): I added a second non-matching value no legacy sidecar carries, the near-miss `video_embeddings.ann_ids` (:94-102). It must raise (:98), and the message must name the value (:101) and `build-ann-index.py` (:102). A rowid/None denylist and a prefix match on ann_id both fail it now. I re-ran the file this round against the pre-change code: 3 failed, 2 passed. All three failures are `DID NOT RAISE RuntimeError` at :87, :98 and :113.\nShape RECOMMENDATION 1 (single-value-pin at :84): taken. The remedy is the same as for the claim CRITICAL; I used the near-miss `video_embeddings.ann_ids` in place of the suggested `ann_id_v0`.\n</findings_addressed>\n\n<rows>\n<row clause=\"C1\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase4.py:78 \u2014 the sidecar a real `--cpu` build-ann-index.py run wrote (control :75 shows its `id_source` is `video_embeddings.ann_id`) passes: `assert_index_matches_embeddings(built, INDEX, DIM, MODEL) is None`. :87/:90/:91 \u2014 the same sidecar with `id_source: \"video_embeddings.rowid\"` raises RuntimeError naming `video_embeddings.rowid` and `build-ann-index.py`. :98/:101/:102 \u2014 the same sidecar with `id_source: \"video_embeddings.ann_ids\"` raises RuntimeError naming `video_embeddings.ann_ids` and `build-ann-index.py`. :113/:116/:117 \u2014 the same sidecar with `id_source` deleted raises RuntimeError naming `<unset>` and `build-ann-index.py`.</assertion>\n<expected>None (no raise) for the ann_id sidecar. A RuntimeError for each of rowid, `video_embeddings.ann_ids` and missing; each message contains the value found (or `<unset>`) and `build-ann-index.py`.</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>The unchanged function never reads `id_source` and accepts all three bad sidecars; I observed this as DID NOT RAISE at :87, :98 and :113. A denylist `if id_source in (\"video_embeddings.rowid\", None): raise` and a prefix match `startswith(ANN_ID_SOURCE)` both accept `video_embeddings.ann_ids`, so :98 reports DID NOT RAISE. `meta.get(\"id_source\", ANN_ID_SOURCE)` defaults the missing key and fails :113. A refusal message without the rebuild script fails :91, :102 and :117; the model matches in these tests, so the model check's message cannot supply the script name. An inverted or always-refusing gate raises at :78.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n</rows>\n\n<answers>\n1. No. The only negative assertion is :131 (`video_embeddings.rowid` not in the model refusal). It is armed by :126, which requires a RuntimeError, and :130, which requires that message to name `other-model`. Deleting the function body makes :126 report DID NOT RAISE.\n2. No. Every expected value is either a literal the test chose and wrote into the sidecar or the build's own output; nothing recomputes production's transformation. Deleting the new `id_source` comparison turns :87, :98 and :113 red, as the pre-change run shows.\n3. No longer. \"Any other\" is now read at three inputs: the legacy rowid, the near-miss `video_embeddings.ann_ids` and a missing key. The near-miss test was added in response to this finding.\n4. No. The only double is `SimpleNamespace(d=8)`, standing in for a faiss index object (third-party). The project-owned build job and schema helper run for real.\n5. Yes. This round's run collected 5 items: the four earlier tests plus the near-miss test. They reuse the existing fixture, helpers and names.\n6. Yes. The build's sidecar shape and the model message come from the earlier probe run. The near-miss case failing with DID NOT RAISE on pre-change code was observed this round. The wording of the post-change message cannot be observed until the phase is built, so the assertions check only for the substrings the clause requires.\n7. Yes. Re-run this round: 3 failed, 2 passed. All three failures are `DID NOT RAISE RuntimeError` at the `pytest.raises` lines :87, :98 and :113, which means the `id_source` check is not built yet. The accept test and the model control pass. The probe tests/tmp/test_probe_42_phase4.py is still on disk and needs deleting; I have no delete tool.\n</answers>",
+    "self_check:tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase4.py": "<rows>\n<row clause=\"C1\">\n<assertion>tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase4.py:77 \u2014 `assert_index_matches_embeddings(built, INDEX, DIM, MODEL) is None` on the unmodified sidecar a real `--cpu` `build-ann-index.py` run wrote (its `id_source` is `video_embeddings.ann_id`, which the control at :74 checks); :89 and :90 \u2014 with `id_source` rewritten to `video_embeddings.rowid`, the RuntimeError message contains `video_embeddings.rowid` and `build-ann-index.py`; :104 and :105 \u2014 with `id_source` deleted, the RuntimeError message contains `<unset>` and `build-ann-index.py`; :118 and :119 \u2014 with the rowid sidecar checked against `other-model`, the RuntimeError names `other-model` and not `video_embeddings.rowid`, so the model check still runs first.</assertion>\n<expected>:77 returns None. This passed in the run. :89/:90 and :104/:105 raise RuntimeError with a message like \"Index ids come from video_embeddings.rowid (or <unset>) but the readers resolve video_embeddings.ann_id. Rebuild the index with build-ann-index.py.\" The plan specifies this message (plan 44, lines 278, 422 and 1372). It cannot be seen until the gate exists: today both cases read \"DID NOT RAISE RuntimeError\" at :86 and :101. :118/:119 get the model refusal \"Index was built from model phase4-model but video_embeddings now holds other-model. Rebuild the index with build-ann-index.py.\", which never mentions the id source. This passed in the run and matches the earlier probe.</expected>\n<wrong_implementation>No id_source check, which is the code as it stands: both refusals read \"DID NOT RAISE RuntimeError\" at :86 and :101 (observed). A missing key defaulted to accepted (`meta.get(\"id_source\", ANN_ID_SOURCE)`): :101 does not raise. A message that prints `None` or `''` instead of `<unset>`: :104 fails. A message that leaves out the source or the rebuild job: :89 or :90 or :105 fails. A check that compares against the wrong value (`video_embeddings.rowid`, or any value that refuses the builder's own output): :77 raises. The id_source check placed before the model check: the mismatch message names `video_embeddings.rowid` and :119 fails.</wrong_implementation>\n</row>\n</rows>\n\n<answers>\n1. No. Every docstring bullet has assertions. The accept half is :77 on the sidecar the real build wrote. Refusal of another source is :89/:90 (names `video_embeddings.rowid` and `build-ann-index.py`). Refusal of a missing source is :104/:105 (names `<unset>` and `build-ann-index.py`). Model check first is :118/:119. Together these cover all of C1: ANN_ID_SOURCE is accepted, any other or missing value is refused, and the message names `build-ann-index.py`.\n2. No. The only negative assertion is :119 (`video_embeddings.rowid` not in the message). The positive :118 (`other-model` in the message) arms it and proves the model-refusal path ran. The acceptance at :77 is backed by the control at :74\u201375, which checks the sidecar really carries the ann_id source and this space. It is also backed by the sibling refusals over the same fixture.\n3. No. No value is compared to itself, and the test never computes the message itself. If the new `if index_id_source != ANN_ID_SOURCE: raise RuntimeError(...)` in `embedding_space.py` is deleted, :86 and :101 go red, and that is the state today.\n4. No. The gate is read at three inputs on the same built sidecar: `video_embeddings.ann_id` (accepted), `video_embeddings.rowid` (refused) and missing (refused). The model ordering is read at a different model.\n5. No. The only double is `SimpleNamespace(d=8)`, which stands in for a `faiss.Index`. That is a third-party type, and the function reads only `.d`. `build-ann-index.py`, `ensure_video_embeddings_schema`, `compute_ann_id` and `assert_index_matches_embeddings` are the real project code.\n6. No. Both imports resolve: `data.ann_ids.compute_ann_id`, `ensure_video_embeddings_schema` and `data.embedding_space.assert_index_matches_embeddings` all exist. The CLI flags are accepted, because the fixture's job exited 0 in all four tests. The handed `--collect-only` summary printed \"no tests\", but the real run says \"collected 4 items\", which matches the four test functions written.\n7. No. The sidecar's `id_source`, `model_name` and `embedding_dim` were seen in the run, where the :74\u201375 controls passed, and in the earlier probe `tests/tmp/test_probe_42_phase4.py`. The model-refusal wording behind :118/:119 was seen the same way (that test passes today). `<unset>` and the gate message cannot be observed before the gate exists. They come from the plan's specification (plan 44 lines 278, 422 and 1372), not from my own reasoning. The implemented phase's run will confirm them. Nothing rewritten. The probe file `tests/tmp/test_probe_42_phase4.py` is still on disk because I have no delete tool, so it needs removing.\n8. Yes. ValidateTests exit status 1: \"2 failed, 2 passed\". The two acceptance/ordering tests are green because today's function accepts every id_source and already runs the model check. The two refusal tests are red.\n9. Yes, it is red for the right reason. `test_refuses_a_rowid_sidecar_naming_the_rowid_source_and_build_ann_index` fails at \"tests/tmp/test_42_ann_ids_b_readers_cutover_phase4.py:86: Failed \u2014 DID NOT RAISE RuntimeError\". `test_refuses_a_sidecar_without_id_source_as_unset_naming_build_ann_index` fails at \":101: Failed \u2014 DID NOT RAISE RuntimeError\". Both got past the fixture: the `ENGINE_PY.exists()` assertion and `result.returncode == 0` held, and the sidecar was rewritten. Both failed on the code under test, which has no id_source gate. No control assertion failed.\n10. The run agrees with the row. :77 and :118/:119 passed as expected. The rowid and missing cases failed by not raising, which is the wrong-implementation reading the row gives for today's code. The message contents in `expected` (`video_embeddings.rowid` / `<unset>` / `build-ann-index.py`) are specified by the plan. They were not observed because the gate does not exist yet, and the run neither confirms nor contradicts them.\n</answers>\n\n<exemptions>\nnone\n</exemptions>",
+    "step_7_implement:4": "<changes>\n### `engine/server/data/embedding_space.py`\n- Imports `ANN_ID_SOURCE` from `data.ann_ids`. That module is stdlib only, and every importer (`api/server.py`, `build-ann-index.py`, `precompute-similar-ann.py`, the system-interpreter tests) already has the server dir on its path, so the import is safe for all of them.\n- `assert_index_matches_embeddings` now checks `id_source` right after the model check and before the dimension checks. It reads `str(meta.get(\"id_source\") or \"\")`. Anything other than `ANN_ID_SOURCE` raises `RuntimeError(\"Index ids come from <found or '<unset>'> but readers resolve video_embeddings.ann_id. Rebuild the index with build-ann-index.py.\")`. The comparison is exact equality, so the near-miss `video_embeddings.ann_ids` is refused. Because the model check runs first, a model mismatch still gets the model message, which does not name the id source.\n- The module docstring gains one paragraph saying why the id source is checked (ADR-0006: a rowid-keyed index would return the wrong videos). The same gate also protects `precompute-similar-ann.py`, which calls this function.\n\n### `engine/server/db/jobs/tests/test-orchestrator-smoke.py`\n- Imports `ANN_ID_SOURCE` next to `ensure_video_embeddings_schema`.\n- In `validate_outputs`, after the `meta_total` check, it raises `RuntimeError` when the sidecar's `id_source` is not `ANN_ID_SOURCE`, and records it as `checks[\"ann_id_source\"]`.\n\n### `tests/active/test_ann_ids.py`, `tests/config.json`\nNot touched. This phase only writes production code. Moving the checkpoint into `tests/active/test_ann_ids.py` and adding it to `tests/config.json` is test-promotion work for the workflow; nothing here needed either file to turn the checkpoint green.\n</changes>\n\n<inner_tests>\nnone\n</inner_tests>\n\n<unanticipated>\nnone\n</unanticipated>\n\n<awaiting_operator>\nnone\n</awaiting_operator>",
+    "step_8_i1_refactor": "<refactors>\nnone. The four phases' production diff is already the smallest form of the cutover. Every reader, the random cache and the index build resolve through `video_embeddings.ann_id`. `ANN_ID_SOURCE` and `assert_video_embeddings_has_ann_id` each live in one place, `data/ann_ids.py`, and the index build, the startup gate and the smoke test import them from there. A grep across the repo for the old names (`exclude_rowid`, `ann_rowids`, `video_rowid`, `random_rowids_count`, `fetch_random_rowids`, `iter_embedding_rows_by_rowids`) finds no live code reference. The only hits are plans and records, the deliberate old-format `random_rowids` probe in tests/active/test_random_cache.py, and tests/tmp checkpoint files.\n</refactors>\n\n<left_out>\nThe private `set_nprobe` in engine/server/db/jobs/precompute-similar-ann.py is a copy of `data.ann.apply_nprobe`. I left it alone for two reasons. It predates this build. And the job cannot import `data.ann` at the top of the module: `data.ann` imports `data.metadata`, which imports `recommendations.keys`, and that lives under `api/`, which the job only adds to `sys.path` inside `main()`. Removing the copy means moving that path setup, which is a structural change for its own step. Also left alone: `_chunk` is copied in data/embeddings.py and data/metadata.py, and `fetch_embeddings_by_ids` has its own embeddings SELECT. Both predate this build and neither is touched by its intent. In random_cache.py I did not swap `populate_random_cache`'s inline `SELECT COUNT(*) ... fetchone()` for `random_ann_ids_count`. The swap adds a `sqlite_master` query to the locked rebuild path, which the 6 s lock-wait test measures, and saves only one line. I also did not rename the job file `precompute-random-rowids.py`: the plan keeps its name and arguments, and the orchestrator and test groups call it by that name. The searches were static (Grep only); I made no test run in this pass.\n</left_out>\n\n<needs_fresh_red>\nnone\n</needs_fresh_red>\n\n<observation>\nStatic searches found no leftover rowid-era names in live code and only pre-existing duplication, none of which can be removed safely inside this build, so the refactor pass changed nothing.\n</observation>",
+    "step_8_triage:0": "<failures>\n### test_similar::test_a_listing_request_without_exactly_nsfw_1_gets_no_flagged_row_where_nsfw_1_gets_some[upnext GET /videos/{id}/similar-missing]\n**Fault: neither the test nor the implementation.** The red came from the state of the checkout's data files during the run. The code is fine.\n\n**The test is built properly.** It sends an nsfw=1 control first, which must return flagged rows. The request without nsfw must then return a non-empty page with no flagged key, checked against whitelist.db's own `nsfw = 1` set. Nothing in it conflicts with this build's requirements, so nothing was retired.\n\n**I could not see its failure message.** `tests/last_test_validation.json` from the same 21:36:41 run records this id as passed, which contradicts the `--compare` line. The operator told me they had fixed a problem since then.\n\n**Observed on the current state:**\n- A fresh Engine was sent the exact sequence for seed `59b6239b-15c6-4bc4-b5e6-6ebac4ea9751@810video.com` (limit 96, seed 11).\n  - nsfw=1 gave 200 with 96 rows, 13 of them flagged.\n  - missing, empty, 0, true and \" 1\" each gave 200 with 96 rows and 0 flagged, in about 0.03 s.\n- The real test then passed in a targeted run (19 passed) and in a run of the whole file (86 passed).\n\n### The six reds `--compare` did not list (already red in the previous record)\nThese were `test_similar` [home] and [random] (500), `test_server` keyed-exclude home (500), `test_frontend_blocks` up-next /recommendations (500), `test_frontend_upnext_pager` (error batch), and `test_random_cache::test_engines_starting_at_once_all_become_healthy` (`no such table: random_ann_ids`).\n\n**Cause: the served `engine/server/db/random-cache.db` was still in the old `random_rowids` shape during the run.** whitelist.db had been migrated (backup `whitelist.db.bak-20261002-213134`) and the index rebuilt with an `ann_id` sidecar at 21:32. The random cache was not rebuilt before the suite started.\n\nWhat followed is what Phase 2's C2 specifies:\n- Each lane's Engine treated the cache as `no_table` and started a background rebuild.\n- That build never finished inside a session. Four orphaned `random-cache.tmp.<pid>.db` files remain from Engines killed mid-build.\n- Meanwhile the random feed fell back to the DB.\n\n**Observed cost of that fallback:** `fetch_random_rows` took 3.25 to 3.35 s on an idle machine, against the 5.0 s `DEFAULT_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`, and it holds `db_lock` throughout.\n\n**Inferred, not observed:** with four Engines rebuilding at once, those requests ran past the deadline. `_handle_similar`'s `except Exception` turned that into the 500 \"Recommendations request failed\". The engine logs that would confirm this are under /tmp, outside the sandbox.\n\n`random-cache.db` now holds 490,348 `random_ann_ids` rows. All six pass in targeted runs:\n- test_similar: 86 passed\n- test_random_cache engines test: 1 passed\n- test_server keyed-exclude test: 1 passed\n- test_frontend_blocks: 2 passed\n- test_frontend_upnext_pager: 1 passed\n\n**Changed:** nothing. No production code, no durable test and no checkpoint was edited, and no test was retired.\n</failures>\n\n<checkpoint_gaps>\nnone\n</checkpoint_gaps>\n\n<correction>\n1. **Checked the failing test first.** It is sound: it has a control, a non-empty assertion, and a flagged-key cross-check against whitelist.db. It does not conflict with any requirement of this build.\n2. **Compared the record with the reported red.** The record shows that id as passed in the same run. It also holds six other reds, all explained by the served random-cache.db still being in the old `random_rowids` shape.\n3. **Checked the served state with existing and new probes.** The whitelist has `ann_id` with 0 nulls across 897,889 rows. The index sidecar has `id_source: video_embeddings.ann_id`, and the startup gate passes. random-cache.db now has `random_ann_ids` (490,348 rows).\n4. **Probed a fresh Engine.** I sent the exact nsfw GET sequence and the home and random feeds. Everything returned 200, with flagged rows only when nsfw=1.\n5. **Measured the DB fallback.** `fetch_random_rows` takes about 3.3 s against the 5 s statement deadline.\n6. **Re-ran every red, as the operator asked.** All passed: test_similar.py in full (86), test_random_cache engines test (1), test_server keyed-exclude test (1), test_frontend_blocks.py (2), test_frontend_upnext_pager.py (1).\n7. **No code, test or checkpoint changes.**\n8. **Cleanup I could not do (no delete tool):**\n   - the orphaned build files `engine/server/db/random-cache.tmp.{855298,855632,856390,858167}.db`;\n   - my probes `tests/tmp/test_probe_step8_nsfw_get.py`, `tests/tmp/test_probe_step8_random_fallback.py` and the log `tests/tmp/probe_step8_engine.log`.\n9. **To prevent a repeat:** rebuild the served random cache with `precompute-random-rowids.py` alongside the whitelist migration and index rebuild, before the next suite run.\n</correction>",
+    "step_8_triage:1": "<failures>\n### All 129 red results (126 errors, 3 failures) across test_blocks, test_dislike_profile, test_dislikes, test_frontend_blocks, test_frontend_upnext_pager, test_random_cache, test_server, test_server_config, test_similar: the Engine refuses to start on the shared dataset, which has not been cut over\n**Cause.** Every error is the session `engine` fixture (`tests/active/conftest.py:140`) or `test_random_cache.py`'s `_cache_variant` failing with \"Engine exited on every start\". The Engine log is outside the sandbox, so I could not read it. Instead I reproduced the startup gate in a probe (`tests/tmp/test_probe_served_data.py`, run through ValidateTests). Against the default served paths it showed:\n- `engine/server/db/whitelist.db` has 897,889 `video_embeddings` rows, and its columns are only `video_id, instance_domain, embedding, embedding_dim, model_name, created_at`. There is no `ann_id`, and no `idx_video_embeddings_ann_id`. Plan A's `migrate-whitelist.py` has never been run on it.\n- The sidecar `whitelist-video-embeddings.faiss.json` has `\"id_source\": \"video_embeddings.rowid\"` (built 2026-10-01, IVFPQ, total 897889).\n- Calling `assert_index_matches_embeddings` on that index and DB raises: `Index ids come from video_embeddings.rowid but readers resolve video_embeddings.ann_id. Rebuild the index with build-ann-index.py.` `server.py:365` calls this at startup, so the process exits 1.\n- `random-cache.db` holds only `random_rowids`. That one is harmless: Phase 2 C2 rebuilds it.\n\nThe three FAILED cases in `test_random_cache.py` have the same root. `test_refresh_on_engine_serves_its_usable_cache...` fails earlier, in `_seed_servable_cache`, which selects `e.ann_id` straight from the shared `whitelist.db` and gets `sqlite3.OperationalError: no such column: e.ann_id`. The other two are variant Engines that exit 1 at the same gate. `test_engines_starting_at_once_all_become_healthy` errors in the session fixture.\n\n**Fault: neither the test nor the implementation.** The tests are built correctly: they start the real Engine on the shared dataset, and they report accurately that it cannot start. The implementation does what Phase 4 C1 requires, which is to refuse an index whose `id_source` is not `ANN_ID_SOURCE`. Plan 42 itself predicts this result. It says \"Engine-backed tests need the shared dataset cut over \u2026 the Engine exits at start until that dataset is migrated and its index rebuilt on `ann_id`. The operator does this before the final full-suite run.\" Under \"Coordination the operator does\" it lists migrate, then `build-ann-index.py`, then start. That cutover has not happened. None of these tests conflicts with a confirmed requirement, so none is retired, and none passes vacuously.\n\n**What I changed:** no production code and no test. Changing either would mean weakening the gate or repointing the tests away from the real dataset.\n\n### test_search_fusion.py \u2014 \"no map entry\" (10 passed)\nThis one is not red. `tests/config.json` has no group for it, so the comparison cannot attribute it. None of this build's phases touched that file or added it. I left `tests/config.json` alone because it is outside this step's files. It needs a map entry, but separately from this build.\n</failures>\n\n<checkpoint_gaps>\nnone. The red comes from a precondition in the environment, not a defect in the code, and no checkpoint could have caught it without breaking the rule it was built on. Phase 3's checkpoint uses explicit tmp `--index-path`/`--meta-path` on purpose, \"so the served index is never touched\". Phase 4's checkpoint calls `assert_index_matches_embeddings` in-process on a tmp sidecar. Both are correct for their clauses. The shared dataset's cutover is named in plan 42 as operator coordination before the final full-suite run, and its impact entry for the shared dataset (worktree `whitelist.db` and index are symlinks to main's live files) says a phase checkpoint on that dataset would need private copies or a coordinated main cutover. So this was planned to wait for the operator, and no checkpoint missed it.\n</checkpoint_gaps>\n\n<correction>\nNo production or test file changed. Nothing retired. No checkpoint touched.\n\n1. I read the suite output. All 129 red results trace to one of two things: the Engine exiting at startup, or a direct `SELECT e.ann_id` on the shared `whitelist.db`.\n2. I wrote and ran the probe `tests/tmp/test_probe_served_data.py`, which confirmed the cause from the data itself. The shared `whitelist.db` has no `ann_id` column. The served sidecar has `id_source = video_embeddings.rowid`. The Phase 4 gate raises the rowid refusal on it.\n3. The suite stays red until the operator does the cutover from plan 42's \"Coordination the operator does\", with the Engine stopped:\n   - (a) `engine/server/db/jobs/migrate-whitelist.py --db engine/server/db/whitelist.db`. This backs up by default and rebuilds `video_embeddings` with `ann_id`.\n   - (b) `build-ann-index.py --db-path engine/server/db/whitelist.db --index-path engine/server/db/whitelist-video-embeddings.faiss --meta-path engine/server/db/whitelist-video-embeddings.faiss.json`. The explicit paths are required, because the job's defaults write `video-embeddings.faiss`, which the Engine does not read, and the refusal would stay.\n   - (c) Start the Engine. The old-shape `random-cache.db` rebuilds itself in the background. The similarity cache needs no rebuild.\n   - (d) Rerun `validate_tests.py --compare`.\n\n   Warning for the operator, from plan 42's impact notes: this worktree's `whitelist.db`, `.faiss` and `.faiss.json` are symlinks to main's live files. Doing the cutover in place therefore rewrites main's live dataset and index. Main's code has no `id_source` gate and still resolves by rowid, so main would then serve wrong or empty similar and search results until plan B merges. The alternative is to give the worktree private copies of the three files before running (a) and (b). That choice belongs to the operator, and I have no shell to run these jobs anyway.\n4. Housekeeping:\n   - The probe is still at `tests/tmp/test_probe_served_data.py`. I have no delete tool, so it needs removing.\n   - Running the probe through ValidateTests overwrote `tests/last_test_validation.json` and `tests/last_test_output.txt` with the probe's single-file run. The suite's own `--compare` record is no longer in those files, and the rerun in step 3(d) will rewrite them.\n</correction>",
